<h2>About</h2>
<p>{{ site.org }} operates Autonomous System <strong>AS{{ site.asn }}</strong> ({{ site.as_name }}) in {{ site.rir }}.
Find records on <a href="{{ site.peeringdb }}">PeeringDB</a>.</p>

{% if (site.prefixes_v4 and (site.prefixes_v4 | length)) or (site.prefixes_v6 and (site.prefixes_v6 | length)) %}
<h3>Announcements</h3>
<ul>
  {% for p in site.prefixes_v4 %}<li>IPv4: <code>{{ p }}</code></li>{% endfor %}
  {% for p in site.prefixes_v6 %}<li>IPv6: <code>{{ p }}</code></li>{% endfor %}
</ul>

<h3>Routing Architecture</h3>
<p>{{ site.org }} uses a geographically distributed failover topology. The announcement structure is:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Supernet</strong>: <code>2602:81c:4000::/44</code> — announced from both edge locations for failover resilience. If a site's specific prefix is withdrawn, traffic routes to this supernet at the alternative location.</li>
  <li><strong>Site-specific /48s</strong>:
    <ul>
      <li>EWR (New Jersey): <code>2602:81c:4000::/48</code></li>
      <li>LAX (Los Angeles): <code>2602:81c:4001::/48</code></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>
<p>More-specific routes (the /48s) are preferred by default routing. When a site performs maintenance or fails, its /48 can be selectively withdrawn while the supernet remains active, routing traffic through the alternative site.</p>
{% endif %}

<h3>Locations</h3>
<table class="table">
  <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>City</th><th>Region</th><th>Country</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    {% for l in site.locations %}
      <tr>
        <td>{{ l.name }}</td>
        <td>{{ l.city }}</td>
        <td>{{ l.region }}</td>
        <td>{{ l.country }}</td>
      </tr>
    {% endfor %}
  </tbody>
</table>

<h3>Contacts</h3>
<ul>
  <li>NOC: <a href="mailto:{{ site.email.noc }}">{{ site.email.noc }}</a></li>
  <li>Peering: <a href="mailto:{{ site.email.peering }}">{{ site.email.peering }}</a></li>
  <li>Abuse: <a href="mailto:{{ site.email.abuse }}">{{ site.email.abuse }}</a></li>
</ul>
